  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAs of this writing, a surface cold front/trough was located \nover central North Dakota and will continue pushing east through\nthe day. Mean southwest flow aloft will keep mid to high clouds\nstreaming in through the day with precipitation chances and \nlower clouds increasing tonight into Sunday. A couple of strung \nout shortwaves will eject from a western CONUS trough which will\nserve as the main focus for rain chances tonight through \nMonday. For now, it appears that rainfall will be more of the \nhit or miss variety tonight and much of Sunday with more\nwidespread high chances arriving late Sunday through Monday. \nThat being said, the northwest should see high chances of rain \nthroughout the aforementioned period and this is the area that \nthe NBM is favoring for the highest totals. NBM 48-hour \nprobabilities suggest a 70 to 90 percent chance for at least a \nhalf inch of rain across most of the northwest and north central\nthrough Monday with the probabilities of an inch or greater \nmaxed out in the 30 to 50 percent range. The best chances will \ngenerally be northwest. \n\nThe cold front will also usher in the coldest temperatures of\nthe year thus far, but maybe not quite as cold as the guidance\nsuggested in previous days. Highs on Sunday will range from the\nmid 40s northwest to the low/mid 60s far southeast. Sunday\nnight's lows will dip into the low to mid 30s northwest and\nnorth central, to the lower 40s south central and southeast. A\nfew readings at or just below freezing will be possible \nnorthwest and north central. That being said, overcast\nconditions may keep temperatures from dipping too low. Given\nthese temperatures Sunday night, some light snow could mix in\nwith the rain across the northwest into Monday morning but \naccumulations are not expected. Highs on Monday will range from\nthe lower 40s northwest to the mid/upper 50s far southeast.\nMonday night could be a little colder than Sunday night but \nwidespread cloud cover will work to keep lows mainly in the mid \nto upper 30s. More readings at or below freezing area possible \nagain north, but clouds may keep the need for freeze headlines \nat bay. \n\nEarly to midweek, an upper low will cutoff over the southwest \nCONUS and model agreement breaks down mid to late week given the\nlow predictability pattern. One solution keeps the low cutoff\nthrough the rest of the work week and one solution has the low\nbeing ingested back into the northern stream. If the latter\nsolution were to verify, we will see better chances for rain \nmid to late week as the southern/northern streams phase over \nMinnesota. However, if the low remains cutoff, split flow will \ndominate and upper ridging will likely suppress precipitation \nchances across the region. As of now, cluster analysis would \nsuggest a ~30 percent chance for continued split flow (drier \nsolution) and maybe a ~70 percent chance for the phased \nsolution (wetter). Even if the wetter solution were to verify, \nit appears that impacts would be minimal at this time. Hopefully\nmodels start to come into better agreement over the next few \ndays. Temperatures may warm slightly mid to late week with highs\nmainly in the 50s (possibly some lower 60s).",
